<p>{% person "anton-c-olson" %}Anton C. Olson{% endperson %} was born in Norway in 1866, one of seven children. His family arrived in Mandan in June 1882.</p>

<figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/olson-steam-shovel.jpg" alt="Anton Olson's Northern Pacific Railroad steam-shovel crew">
  <figcaption>Anton Olson's Northern Pacific Railroad steam-shovel crew</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Olson began working for the Northern Pacific Railroad as a water boy in March 1883. After turning 17 the following year, he became a general laborer laying track. From 1885 to 1887, he worked on a steam-shovel crew. He survived being buried beneath tons of sand and rock when his coworkers frantically dug him out.</p>

<p>Olson continued working for the railroad throughout his career, advancing from machinist's helper in 1887 to general laborer in 1890 and locomotive fireman in 1892.</p>

<p>From 1900 through 1908, he was responsible for clearing all train wrecks between Fargo and Glendive, work that sometimes required recovering bodies. In 1908, he became a locomotive inspector. He retired in March 1936 at age 70 after completing 50 years of continuous service with the Northern Pacific.</p>

<p>Olson married {% person "anna-wold-olson" %}Anna Wold{% endperson %} in Mandan on March 25, 1894. The Reverend Gustad officiated, and the couple had five children.</p>

<figure>
  <img src="/assets/images/blue-grass-siding-train-wreck.jpg" alt="A 1920s Northern Pacific derailment at Blue Grass Siding">
  <figcaption>Northern Pacific derailment at Blue Grass Siding in the 1920s</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Olson sang in the church choir and served on the building committee of the Lutheran church at 225 Ninth Avenue SW, constructed in 1891.</p>

<p>Blue Grass Siding was three miles southwest of New Salem. It was reportedly named for broad-bladed, blue-colored bunchgrass said to have been brought from Kentucky by soldiers serving under General Custer.</p>
